course: dictionary
lesson: 15324
title: Same thing, different name
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «sneaker» has two meanings, so read the sentence around it to find the right one. «Ben laced up his new sneakers before the football match.» Laced up and football match are the clues: sneakers are shoes for sport.
explain: In the USA, Canada and Australia, sneakers are soft sports shoes with rubber soles. In the UK, people call the same shoes trainers, so a child in Ohio and a child in Manchester may be talking about one thing with two names.
explain: The second meaning is a person who sneaks. «The sneaker tiptoed past the sleeping guard.» Tiptoed is the clue: a sneaker moves quietly so nobody notices. Always swap your meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Sneaker is a noun. You can have one sneaker or a pair of sneakers, and it is usually used in the plural.
fact: «Sneakers» means soft sports shoes with a rubber sole, in the USA, Canada and Australia.
fact: In the UK, the same shoes are called «trainers». In Ireland, people often say «runners».
fact: They are called sneakers because rubber soles are quiet, so you can sneak about.
fact: «Sneaker» can also mean a person who sneaks, someone who moves quietly so that no one sees them.
exercise: «Jake laced up his sneakers and raced to the park.» What are sneakers here? | Shoes with high heels for parties | *Soft sports shoes with rubber soles | Long, cold gloves for winter | Small birds that sing at dawn
exercise: In the UK, people call the same shoes what? | Slippers | Wellies | *Trainers | Gloves
exercise: «The sneaker crept behind the shed to steal the apples.» What does sneaker mean here? | A type of apple | *A person who sneaks | A soft sports shoe | A garden tool
test: Why are sneakers called sneakers, according to the dictionary? | They are very fast on the track | They were first made from snow | They were invented by a famous explorer | *Their rubber soles are quiet, so you can sneak about
test: Aoife in Dublin says, «My runners are muddy after the match.» What are runners? | Muddy paths across the fields | Water bottles for football | *Sports shoes, like sneakers | People who race in the park
exam: «The sneaker tiptoed past the sleeping guard, but the dog began to bark.» Which clue shows that a sneaker is a person, not a shoe? | sleeping | the dog began to bark | *tiptoed past the sleeping guard | sneaker
